> > In practice, any character with a reasonable XL (i.e.  14+) will be of
> > high enough level to meet basically anything, since once you're on your
> > way out with the trinket, the DL factor is always the depth of Moloch's
> > Sanctum, which will be something on the order of 45-50, giving an XL 14
> > character a mongen difficulty of 29-32.  An XL 30 character will face a
> > mongen difficulty of 37-40.
> > 
> > The most powerful randomly generable monsters are
> > arch-liches (difficulty 29) and Archons
> > (difficulty 26).
> 
> Umm, without looking at the code, I'd guess the
> implementation would be that your "mongen" value
> sets the _center_ of the range of selectable
> monsters.

It actually sets the upper limit of it. The lower limit is (for the
ascension run up to DL 1) a straight Sanctum-depth/6, your XL not
taken into consideration. So there ought to be no difference
whatsoever in the variety of monsters eligible to be created, since
higher XL is just extending the potential range to allow monsters that
aren't there to be allowed.

(It will make a difference in the Endgame, where the lower limit
becomes ((Sanctum-depth + (XL/2))/6). But the main worry there is the
preplaced monsters, not the randomly-created ones.)

> If this guess is right, I'd be interested to know if
> the monster selection is uniform across the range,
> or normally distributed across the range.

It's uniform once you adjust for the other influences: namely, monster
types' individual generation frequency settings, and a bias towards
creating monsters coaligned with the alignment of the branch or
special level you're on, if it has one. (Also, some monsters are
eliminated from consideration for reasons other than their difficulty
setting, such as lower-case monsters on the Rogue level.)

